    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dantidurga.jpg" alt="Dantidurga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga">Dantidurga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rashtrakutas_Coins_25-07-23_15-44-48-445.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shraman revival, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty, often called the Imperial Pratiharas, emerged in the eighth century in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in the region of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. The name joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurjars">Gurjaras</a>, a people and later a social group widely present in the region, whose origins are debated, with the title <em>pratihara</em>, ‘doorkeeper’; the dynasty’s own Gwalior inscription traced its descent from Lakshmana, who served as doorkeeper to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>. Several Pratihara and Gurjara lineages existed, including the Pratiharas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandore">Mandavyapura</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> and the Gurjaras of Nandipuri in Gujarat, but the imperial line begins with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagabhata-i">Nagabhata I</a>, whose base is variously placed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> or in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalor</a> region. In the 720s and 730s Arab governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junayd-painter">Junayd</a>, raided deep into western India, and the Gwalior inscription praises Nagabhata I for destroying the armies of a powerful foreign king, a claim usually linked to the repulse of these attacks around 738. His successors were not yet secure: a later Rashtrakuta inscription claims that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga">Dantidurga</a> made a Gurjara ruler serve as his doorkeeper at Ujjain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja">Vatsaraja</a> (r. c. 775–805) extended Pratihara power into central India, and the Jain work <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuvalayamala">Kuvalayamala</a></em>, completed at Jalor in 778, names him as the reigning king. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagabhata-ii">Nagabhata II</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayudha-dynasty">Ayudha dynasty</a> around 816 and made it the imperial capital.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kannauj"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kannauj.jpg" alt="Kannauj" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goddess_Annapurna_Temple_,tirwa_town,stateUttar_Pradesh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nature lusty vikash, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pratihara empire drew its wealth from the agriculture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab">Ganges–Yamuna Doab</a> and the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, where wheat, barley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> were grown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, also called Mahodaya, was among the largest cities of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning and the prize of rival dynasties. Land grants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and temples recorded on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates extended cultivation and created local landed elites. The empire’s coinage consisted mainly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and billon drammas descended from Sasanian models; the coins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihira-bhoja">Mihira Bhoja</a> bear the title <em>Adivaraha</em>, identifying the king with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>’s boar incarnation. Historians have long debated whether the relative scarcity of coins in this period reflects a decline of trade and towns, but merchants and markets clearly continued to operate, and Arab sources describe the kingdom as rich. Religious life was diverse. Pratihara rulers were devotees of Vishnu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and the goddess Bhagavati, and Jain communities flourished under their rule: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a> temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osian">Osian</a> in Rajasthan is dated by a later inscription to the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja">Vatsaraja</a>. Temple building in this period at Osian, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bateshwar-hindu-temples">Bateshwar</a> group near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morena-political-party">Morena</a> and at Gwalior laid foundations for the later temple styles of western and central India. The court of Kannauj supported the poet and critic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajashekhara">Rajashekhara</a>, author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> play <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karpuramanjari">Karpuramanjari</a></em> and the treatise on poetics <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavyamimamsa">Kavyamimamsa</a></em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha.jpg" alt="Dhruva Dharavarsha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha">Dhruva Dharavarsha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Kannada_inscription_(780_AD)_from_Naregal_of_Rashtrakuta_King_Dhruva_Dharavarsha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">E Hultzsch (Eugen Julius Theodor Hultzsch), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pratiharas were famous above all for their cavalry. The Arab merchant whose account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was written down in 851 described the king of the Juzr, as Arab writers called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurjars">Gurjaras</a>, as having more horsemen than any other Indian ruler and as the most hostile of the Indian kings to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and the geographer al-Masudi, who visited India around 915, reported that the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> kept large armies on each of his frontiers. For two centuries the dynasty fought what historians call the tripartite struggle for control of Kannauj and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> with the Pala Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and the Rashtrakuta dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja">Vatsaraja</a> defeated the Pala king Dharmapala but was routed by the Rashtrakuta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhruva-dharavarsha">Dhruva</a> around 786, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagabhata-ii">Nagabhata II</a>, after taking Kannauj and defeating Dharmapala near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munger">Monghyr</a>, was in turn defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iii">Govinda III</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihira-bhoja">Mihira Bhoja</a> (r. c. 836–885) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendrapala-i">Mahendrapala I</a> (r. c. 885–910) the Pratiharas gained the upper hand, extending their rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. On the western frontier the empire held the Arab principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> in check; Arab writers report that the rulers of Multan threatened to destroy its famous sun-temple idol whenever a Pratihara army approached. The great defeats came from the south: the Rashtrakuta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra-iii">Indra III</a> sacked Kannauj around 916, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> took the city in 1018.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harsha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harsha.jpg" alt="Harsha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harshavardhana_Circa_AD_606-647.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Pratihara kings bore the imperial titles <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharajadhiraja">Maharajadhiraja</a></em> and <em>Parameshvara</em>, and at its height their empire was the largest in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> since that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>. It was a layered structure. The core territories around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab">Doab</a> were administered through provinces called <em>bhukti</em>, divided into <em>mandala</em> and <em>vishaya</em>, with royal officials collecting revenue and recording grants. Beyond this core the emperors ruled through feudatory dynasties, the <em>samantas</em>, who governed their own lands, paid tribute and sent troops; among them were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chahamanas</a> of Shakambhari, the Guhilas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandela-dynasty">Chandela dynasty</a>, the Kalachuris and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a>. Royal officers also held important fortresses: Alla, commander of the fort of Gwalior under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihira-bhoja">Mihira Bhoja</a>, recorded his works in inscriptions of 875–876, including the Chaturbhuj temple inscription. Mihira <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhoja-paramara">Bhoja</a>’s long reign of almost fifty years gave the empire its greatest stability, and his Gwalior inscription set out the dynasty’s genealogy and achievements. Succession was generally from father to son but was sometimes contested, as after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendrapala-i">Mahendrapala I</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahipala-i-pratihara">Mahipala I</a> had to overcome a rival. The reliance on feudatories allowed the empire to expand rapidly but also meant that its unity depended on the emperor’s military prestige. When that prestige faded after 916, the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> turned into independent kingdoms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khajuraho"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khajuraho.jpg" alt="Khajuraho" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khajuraho">Khajuraho</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_Khajuraho.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PLBechly, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Gurjara-Pratiharas are often credited by historians with halting the eastward advance of Arab power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in 711–712, Arab armies raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, but from the mid-eighth century Muslim rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> remained confined to Sindh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> for almost three centuries, and the strength of the Pratiharas, along with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> and Rashtrakutas, was a major reason. Within India the dynasty made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> the symbolic capital of the north, so that control of the city became the measure of imperial ambition for the Palas, Rashtrakutas and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gahadavala-dynasty">Gahadavala dynasty</a>. Many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> dynasties that dominated northern India in the tenth to twelfth centuries, including the Chandelas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khajuraho">Khajuraho</a>, the Paramaras of Malwa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chahamanas</a> who later ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>, rose as Pratihara feudatories. The temples of the Pratihara period at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osian">Osian</a>, Gwalior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bateshwar-hindu-temples">Bateshwar</a> contributed to the formation of the northern temple traditions that culminated at Khajuraho, and the court culture of Kannauj produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajashekhara">Rajashekhara</a>, one of the most influential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> critics. Later bardic traditions made the Pratiharas one of the four fire-born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnikula">Agnikula</a> Rajput clans, a legend that shows their place in the historical memory of the Rajputs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krishna-iii.jpg" alt="Krishna III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Kannada_inscription_dated_Shaka_868_(c.944-45_AD)_of_Rashtrakuta_empire_King_Krishna_III.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jugaari cross, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of the empire began with the Rashtrakuta invasion led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra-iii">Indra III</a>, who sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> around 916. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahipala-i-pratihara">Mahipala I</a> regained his capital, apparently with the help of the Chandela king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>, and his court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajashekhara">Rajashekhara</a> still praised him as a world-conqueror, but the defeat showed the empire’s vulnerability and broke the feudatories’ respect. Over the following decades the Chandelas took the great fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinjar-fort">Kalanjara</a>, the Paramaras established themselves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukyas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and the Kalachuris in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and further Rashtrakuta campaigns under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a> weakened the north. By the late tenth century the Pratiharas ruled little more than the region around Kannauj. In December 1018 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> marched on the city; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajyapala">Rajyapala</a> abandoned it without a fight, and Kannauj and its temples were plundered. For this submission the Chandela king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidyadhara-chandela-dynasty">Vidyadhara</a> led a coalition that killed Rajyapala around 1019. His successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trilochanapala-shahi-dynasty">Trilochanapala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yashahpala">Yashahpala</a> ruled a small territory around Kannauj and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a>, and Yashahpala’s inscription of 1036 is the last record of the dynasty. Kannauj passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gahadavala-dynasty">Gahadavala dynasty</a> around 1090, and the former Pratihara lands were divided among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> kingdoms that had grown up as its feudatories.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Gurjara-Pratiharas built the largest empire of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, held Arab power in check in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> for generations, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> the prize of the tripartite struggle, while their feudatories became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> dynasties that ruled the north after them.</em></p>

